Rule Error name is a GUID 400 <code>InvalidRoleAssignmentId</code> (verify) <code>properties.roleDefinitionId</code> present and resolvable (built-in by trailing GUID, or custom at scope or an ancestor) 400 <code>RoleDefinitionDoesNotExist</code> (verify) <code>properties.principalId</code> present and a GUID 400 <code>InvalidPrincipalId</code> (verify). There is no directory, so nothing beyond GUID shape. <code>Current != nil</code> and principalId, roleDefinitionId or scope changed 400 <code>RoleAssignmentUpdateNotPermitted</code> (verify) Another assignment at the same scope with the same principalId and role GUID, under a different name 409 <code>RoleAssignmentExists</code> <p>Shared resolver, used by Validate and injected into the Enforcer as its <code>RoleResolver</code> (ADR-0008):</p> <pre><code>// ResolveRoleDefinition finds a built-in by GUID, else a custom definition at scope or an ancestor.
func (h *Handler) ResolveRoleDefinition(id string, scope arm.ID) (map[string]any, bool, error)
</code></pre> <p>Custom definitions stay at their legacy keys until roleDefinitions migrate (gate G3). The resolver reads those keys lock-free, through <code>Store.Lookup</code> and <code>Store.Scan</code>, so backend errors propagate as 500. The resolver is the only code that knows where definitions live. Migrating roleDefinitions later changes its body, not its callers.</p>
